--- body ---

## Context

The base library must enforce recorded admission outcomes while leaving semantic classification to agent judgment. This replaces the conditional survey that could close with scoring-only enforcement; native memory must not be an unrestricted canonical writer.

Design baseline: v7. v6 was merged by PR #903 and is frozen at `docs/2026-09-10-dragon-memory-design-v6.md`; v7 closes the acceptance review's four §12.1 conditions and settles the nomenclature, leaving the architecture and this ticket's scope unchanged. See `docs/2026-09-10-dragon-memory-design.md`, `docs/2026-09-10-dragon-memory-design-nomenclature.md` and `docs/2026-09-11-memory-implementation-plan.md`. This planning change implements nothing and closes no work.

## Relevant files

`skills/memory/SKILL.md`; `rules/knowledge-hints.md`; `tickets/AGENTS.md`; package/CLI from 0911.

## Actions

1. Require a recorded canonical-owner routing outcome: existing decision records, STATE/ticket, reusable artefact, knowledge hint, experiential memory, or unresolved.
2. Validate admitted bodies through the common library. Reject malformed predicates; preserve explicitly unverified applicability where the consuming environment cannot evaluate it.
3. Document branch-local acceptance: author's working context may be uncommitted; other readers use committed branch state through normal Git integration.
4. Require a recorded audience/scope suitability judgment before canonical admission; unresolved audience or scope prevents publication into that destination without forcing an automatic confirmation loop. Keep this judgment distinct from structural validation and truth. Do not build native-note import or bidirectional synchronisation.
5. Provide the common lifecycle mutation path: explicit retraction/supersession updates status, moves the local body into inactive/, adds a readable notice and repairs relative links in one committed change. UUID references remain stable; redirects cannot become active memories. Use expected revisions and refuse to overwrite uncommitted changes.

## Test

Mechanical fixtures reject missing routing outcome and malformed schema, accept a qualified unknown, and avoid an automatic user-confirmation loop. Missing or unresolved audience/scope cannot publish into the destination. Exercise post-migration retraction/supersession, stale expected revisions, uncommitted edits, historical links and redirect exclusion. Behavioural routing examples are labelled evaluation cases, not deterministic semantic guarantees.

## Verification

- [ ] Run the focused fixtures and show they detect the relevant failure.
- [ ] Run applicable repository checks; record commands, results and limitations.
- [ ] Confirm the implementation matches this scope and v7, including explicit capability limits.

## Invariants

- Canonical bodies and provenance remain reviewable; memory cannot override decisions.
- No mandatory residency, automatic generalisation, new native-memory modes or policy duplication.
- Use the common library and normal Git integration; no runtime implementation is part of the planning PR.

## Exit criteria

- [ ] CLI/library admission emits structured outcomes and provenance.
- [ ] Decision-shaped input is not silently made authoritative by a score.
- [ ] False/unknown applicability policy matches v7; no universal true-at-write requirement.
- [ ] v7 §12.1 predicate-safety row: an operator outside 0911's closed subset, a path outside its declared root, or a payload that would have a side effect if interpreted is rejected at entry and evaluates *unknown*, with a sentinel the payload would create verifiably absent afterwards. The sentinel is the positive control; without it the row cannot distinguish a predicate that did not execute from one that was never looked at.
- [ ] That fixture exists before 0920 commits any `shared-snapshot/`, since a replica carries predicates authored in another project.
